WEBVTT

00:00:00.326 --> 00:00:05.478
Welcome to vNAAATS, Modern Oceanic Control for the Nat Region on VATSIM.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:20.480
vNAAATS modernizes how oceanic traffic is controlled across the NAT region. It replaces the old tools with a flight data display, a plugin for EuroScope, and a server that links them together, so everything stays in step between controllers.

00:00:21.414 --> 00:00:28.070
You can run the flight data display in your browser, or install FDD for Windows, a small app that opens it for you.

00:00:28.358 --> 00:00:33.510
The EuroScope plugin passes flights and clearances between the display and your radar screen.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:46.918
The vNAAATS flight data display shows every aircraft entering the NAT region on one page, its track, level, Mach and estimates. Pending requests that need action sit at the top and the cleared flights are listed beside them.

00:00:48.262 --> 00:00:55.270
At vNAAATS, pilots now ask for their oceanic route with a request for clearance or RCL, 30 to 90 minutes before the entry point.

00:00:55.718 --> 00:01:05.382
It is a request, not a clearance. The controller processes it and the pilot is told to fly the current flight plan or is amended by a TC and to squawk 2,000 at the entry.

00:01:07.014 --> 00:01:10.438
If you would like more help, AutoRCL sends the replies for you.

00:01:10.694 --> 00:01:20.710
A red star on the tag shows that a request has been sent, even when the aircraft is still 90 minutes away and outside your visibility range. So don't worry, it has been sent.

00:01:21.606 --> 00:01:26.086
Not every pilot has CPDLC and it is not a requirement on VATSIM.

00:01:26.374 --> 00:01:33.190
If they are not connected to Hoppie, the request goes as a plain message in EuroScope, and that track carries on as before.

00:01:33.574 --> 00:01:35.846
Requests under 30 minutes are for voice.

00:01:36.774 --> 00:01:48.966
The vNAAATS handoff helper is a helping hand, not a hand on the controls. In busy periods, when entry traffic loads into the NAT region, it reminds you to hand off, so no aircraft slips past its boundary.

00:01:50.022 --> 00:01:56.870
This is a change of process for controllers and for pilots alike, and it modernizes Oceanic on VATSIM for everyone.

00:01:58.534 --> 00:02:13.632
To get started with VNAZ, get your free key at vNAAATS.com/get-key. It lasts 90 days. Install FDD for Windows or use the browser and paste the key once. Then connect to VATSIM on an oceanic position and the display opens by itself.

00:02:15.014 --> 00:02:18.694
Watch the how-to videos at vnayats.com/training

00:02:19.014 --> 00:02:19.745
vNAAATS

